<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Bahdinan emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1376 CE – 1843 CE (approx.) · capital Amadiya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish emirate in the mountains north of Mosul, centred on the clifftop town of Amadiya, that ruled for over four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bahdinan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> claimed descent from the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> through an ancestor named Baha al-Din, who gave the dynasty its name. They ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadiya">Amadiya</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bit-zamani">Amedi</a>), a town on a sheer rock plateau in the mountains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, from at least the late 14th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> governed Kurdish tribes, Assyrian Christian and Jewish villages and Yazidi communities in the valleys between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a>. After the Ottoman conquest of the region in the 16th century they became hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, providing troops for its wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn-wali">Sultan Husayn Wali</a> was a notable ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadiya">Amadiya</a> was known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholars.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by internal feuds and attacks by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/soran-emirate">Soran emirate</a> in the 1830s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was abolished during Ottoman centralisation in the early 1840s, its lands placed under the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1376 CE – 1843 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.08%;width:9.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1376 CE – 1843 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amadiya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sultan Husayn Wali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish (Kurmanji)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bahdinan, Badinan, Emirate of Amadiya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1610&amp;focus=bahdinan-emirate" aria-label="Bahdinan emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5783 980 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bahdinan emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6103 1236h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6103 1236h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=1610&amp;focus=bahdinan-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

